  "step_2": "The individual compares the two lotteries by focusing on the prize amounts (2400 vs 2500) and their probabilities (33% vs 34%). They note that the difference in probabilities is only 1%, while the difference in outcomes is 100 Euros, which they interpret as a relatively larger difference (speculating it's more than 1%, perhaps 5%). The individual justifies choosing Lottery B by stating a preference for the higher reward despite the slightly lower chance, because the probability difference is considered negligible. The deliberation centers on trade-offs between outcome magnitude and probability, with an emphasis on the smallness of the probability difference relative to the outcome difference. No mention is made of an average or reference point derived from all outcomes, nor is there explicit consideration of whether outcomes exceed a common benchmark.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires computing an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes (≈1633.33) and comparing each lottery based on the probability of exceeding this level. The individual does not mention or imply such a reference point. Instead, their reasoning is based on direct comparison of the two lotteries’ payoffs and probabilities, focusing on the relative size of the differences. While both the reason and the individual favor Lottery B, the justification differs fundamentally: the individual emphasizes the small probability difference and the relatively large payoff difference, whereas the decision reason relies on a specific computation of a neutral aspiration level and evaluates which lottery more frequently exceeds it. There is no evidence the individual computed or used such a benchmark. The reasoning process described is more aligned with a heuristic trade-off (probability vs. payoff) than with evaluating outcomes relative to an aspiration level. Therefore, the decision reason does not appear to have been used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
